IP quality functionality is informed by and adapted from:

  IPQuality
  https://github.com/xykt/IPQuality
  Audited revision: 44a55baec6cd
  License: GNU Affero General Public License v3.0

Adapted concepts include the multi-provider coverage, provider field mappings,
network/company type normalization, factor matrix, and provider-specific risk
bands for IP2Location, Scamalytics, AbuseIPDB, IPQualityScore, and DB-IP.

ecs reimplements these capabilities in Go around a versioned report model. It
adds official API-key routing, preserves provider failures and disagreements,
supports local JSON/Markdown/HTML rendering, and never averages unlike
scores into an opaque overall score.

The following IPQuality behavior and assets are intentionally not included:

  - sponsor or advertising output and sponsor images;
  - run counters, telemetry, or hit endpoints;
  - automatic online report generation or report upload;
  - dependency installation;
  - media-unlock and mail-provider test code.

Provider data remains subject to each provider's own terms, quotas, and privacy
policy. IPQuality and the provider names are trademarks or project names of
their respective owners; no endorsement is implied.

Optional ecs-tools packages are separate-process aggregates, not Go code linked
into ecs. Each platform target package carries its own manifest.json,
LICENSE, NOTICE, and CI-populated LICENSES/ directory. The manifest schema
supports unknown/unavailable version and license fields until CI has built and
audited a real artifact. This notice does not claim that a release asset or
tool version exists merely because a manifest example exists.

The main Go module remains standard-library-only. Native Windows support uses
the Windows API through the Go standard library and does not add a third-party
Go dependency.

On Unix, when run.sh needs pinned zstd, NPB EP/FT, pinned OpenSSL, official
STREAM, fio, iperf3, NextTrace Tiny, or ping, it uses the platform-matched
ecs-tools tar.gz artifact for fixed-contract components and for any missing
general tool. On Windows Server 2022+ x64, the release target is
windows_amd64 and the main and tools assets are respectively
ecs_windows_amd64.zip and ecs-tools_windows_amd64.zip. The Windows tools
asset contains only zstd, NPB EP, NPB FT, OpenSSL, STREAM, and fio; its
build-time MSYS2 UCRT64 inputs are pinned in tools/lock.json and are not a
Go dependency or a runtime tool asset. License notices for the six tools are
generated from their upstream sources into the package LICENSES/ directory.
The Windows asset does not support sysbench or iperf3; Ookla is unsupported
there as well.

NextTrace Tiny and the pinned ping build are Linux-only: the FreeBSD tool
packages omit both and use the base-system ping and traceroute instead. The
Windows package omits both; native Win32 system probes and native Win32 ICMP
are implemented by ecs itself, and fio's Windows native engine is windowsaio.
The Windows route and backtrace modules remain unsupported because NextTrace
has not passed an independent genuine Windows network gate. The artifact is
accepted only after checks against the Bundle Release's checksums.txt, then
only the requested regular executable members are unpacked into the temporary
run directory and exposed through the temporary PATH (or its Windows
PowerShell equivalent).
When the zstd benchmark is selected, run.sh downloads and
extracts the corpus asset from the Bundle Release and verifies its archive
against the Bundle Release's checksums.txt; the zstd probe verifies its
fixed size and SHA-256 immediately before use. APT/Packagecloud is reserved for an explicitly selected Ookla
adapter and is not a source for these general missing tools.

The ecs-tools set intentionally excludes the Ookla speedtest client. Ookla is
an explicit external adapter with its own terms and privacy policy: it is part
of the full default profile, omitted from the standard default profile, and
can be explicitly selected from any profile on supported Unix targets. It is
unsupported on Windows and is never part of ecs-tools.
System-provided ping likewise has no single project-wide license: the
applicable notice is the distribution package actually used. On FreeBSD the
latency and routing probes run the base-system /sbin/ping and
/usr/sbin/traceroute owned by the installed FreeBSD release, so their notice
is the operating system's, not a bundled tool package's. On Windows, the
latency probe calls native Win32 ICMP APIs rather than a third-party ping
binary.

